=3Dformat&amp;fit=3Dmax&amp;s=3D94cf66e4a2f9481a746a27150d287dd6" alt=3D"Fr=
ee Range chicken-egg farm. Park Farm, Syleham Eye, Suffolk. The farm is run=
 by Philip &amp; Christine Greenacre. There are a total of 20,000 chickens =
here, with 8000 kept in the shed photographed and another 12000 at a nearby=
 shed. The birds are kept in colonies of 4000 (this shed has 4000 birds in =
each half) and inside the shed there is enough space for there to be 9 bird=
s per square meter if they were all enclosed inside, as per  the directives=
 handed down by Freedom Foods and DEFRA. This operation was started 2 years=
 ago after Philip and Christine decided that their arable farming was no lo=
nger proving profitable enough, although they do still have 120 acres of ar=
able farmed land, as well as a pig farming operation. The chickens here are=
 known as 'Hyline' and are bred by special chicken rearers in Shropshire. T=
hey are brought to the farm at 16 weeks old and are kept here until they ar=
e 54 weeks old.  The egg-yield is on average 93% of the 20000 chickens. All=
 of the eggs are supplied to Marks &amp; Spencers within 3 days of being la=
id. In the UK, 27% of the egg market is now free range. By 2012 it is expec=
ted that the percentage will reach 75%. The birds are free to wander in and=
 out of the shed as they please. There are 400 birds per acre of range-land=
, making a total of 20 acres of surrounding fields for these 8000 chickens =
to range in. The chicken feed is cereal based. The start-up costs for the s=
heds and protective electric fencing amounted to roughly  23 per bird, not =
including running-costs or the cost of the birds themselves. If avian flu r=
eaches the UK, then it may be possible to protect these birds by keeping th=
em enclosed in the sheds, but that would alter the harvest from free-range =
to 'barn' eggs. If any signs of avian flu are detected in the chickens then=
 they will most certainly be culled." class=3D"mobile-width-122px"></a></td=
